# Permutation Puzzle — From Heuristics to Search

After setting up the basic permutation puzzle environment, the next challenge was no longer the game itself, but designing agents that could actually reason about the feedback.

The environment was intentionally kept simple. A hidden permutation is generated, an agent submits a permutation as its guess, and the environment returns a single piece of information:

> **How many elements are currently in the correct position?**

For example, if the target is:

A B C D

and the agent guesses:

A C B D

the feedback is:

2

The agent knows that two positions are correct, but it does not know which two.

That distinction became the central difficulty of the experiment.

---

## 1. The First Problem: Feedback Is Not Local

The first versions of the agents were based on relatively intuitive strategies.

The idea was to make a change, observe whether the score improved, and use that change to decide what to do next.

This seemed reasonable for a small search space.

With four elements there are only:

4! = 24 possible permutations, so even relatively weak strategies can appear effective.

However, the feedback does not identify which action caused the improvement.

Suppose an agent changes several positions and receives a higher score.

It cannot safely conclude that one particular position is now correct.

The improvement may have resulted from several positional relationships changing simultaneously.

This exposed an important weakness in the original approach:

> An improvement in the global score is not equivalent to evidence that a specific position is correct.

The problem was therefore not simply "guess better."

The agent needed some form of memory and awareness of previous states.

---

# Baseline 1 — Random Agent

The first baseline was intentionally simple.

The Random Agent generates a random permutation at every step and does not use feedback to improve its strategy.

The only state it maintains is a set of previously attempted guesses, preventing it from submitting exactly the same permutation twice.

Random permutation
        ↓
Receive score
        ↓
Ignore score
        ↓
Generate another unseen permutation


This agent serves an important purpose: it establishes a lower-level baseline against which more intelligent strategies can be compared.

It also gives a useful reference for understanding how much improvement is actually coming from reasoning rather than simply from exploring the search space.

---

# Baseline 2 — Systematic Agent

The next approach attempted to introduce memory.

The Systematic Agent stores:

(guess, feedback)

for every previous attempt.

Before generating a new guess, it checks whether the candidate is consistent with all previous observations.

For a candidate permutation, the agent calculates how many positions match each previous guess:

The candidate is rejected if this number does not match the feedback previously received.

Conceptually:

Previous guess       Feedback
A B C D              2
B A C D              1

            ↓

Candidate must satisfy BOTH constraints


This was a major improvement over simply reacting to the latest score.

Instead of asking:

> "Did my last change make things better?"

the agent asks:

> "Is this candidate compatible with everything I have learned so far?"

This introduced the first real form of constraint-based reasoning into the project.

However, candidate generation was still largely random. The agent sampled permutations until it found one satisfying the accumulated constraints.

This meant that although the agent was reasoning about validity, it was not yet reasoning about which valid guess would be most useful.

---

# Baseline 3 — Logic Agent

The Logic Agent was an attempt to make the search more directed.

It maintained:

* The complete history of guesses and feedback.
* A set of previously attempted permutations.
* The highest score observed so far.
* A current_best permutation.

Whenever a new score exceeded the previous best, the corresponding guess became the new reference point.

Future candidates were then generated by making a small number of swaps to the current best permutation.

Current best
     ↓
Swap 1–3 pairs
     ↓
Check consistency
     ↓
Check whether already tried
     ↓
Submit candidate

This was intended to exploit a useful intuition:

> If a permutation has produced a relatively high score, nearby permutations may contain useful information.

The agent therefore searched locally around its best-known guess before falling back to global random sampling.

---

## The problem with the Logic Agent

This approach exposed another weakness.

The agent had memory, but memory alone did not produce good reasoning.

It could remember previous guesses and reject inconsistent candidates, but the local search process could still become ineffective after several steps.

The underlying problem was that a high-scoring guess does not necessarily provide a reliable "direction" toward the solution.

A permutation with score 6 is not necessarily more useful as a starting point than another permutation with score 5.

More importantly, changing a few positions around that guess does not guarantee that the resulting candidates will provide useful information.

The agent was becoming more systematic, but it was still relying heavily on local heuristics.

---

# Hybrid Agent

The Hybrid Agent combined the strongest ideas from the previous approaches.

It retained:

* Historical constraints.
* Memory of attempted guesses.
* A best-known guess.
* Local mutation through swaps.
* Global random sampling as a fallback.

Instead of immediately selecting a single candidate, it first generates a candidate pool.

The pool is created in two stages:

1. Local candidates

If a best guess exists, the agent generates nearby permutations by applying a small number of swaps.

2. Global candidates

If the local search does not produce enough candidates, the agent samples additional random permutations from the broader search space.

Every candidate must satisfy the historical constraints and must not have been tried previously.

This gave the agent considerably more flexibility than the earlier approaches.

---

# Introducing Information-Based Selection

The most significant addition to the Hybrid Agent was an attempt to move beyond simply finding a valid candidate.

Once a pool of possible candidates exists, the agent tries to select the candidate that can produce the greatest variety of feedback outcomes.

For each potential guess, it simulates what feedback would be produced against the candidate pool.

For example, a candidate might produce:

Feedback 0 → 12 candidates
Feedback 1 → 25 candidates
Feedback 2 → 18 candidates
Feedback 3 → 7 candidates

Another candidate might produce:

Feedback 0 → 20 candidates
Feedback 1 → 20 candidates
Feedback 2 → 15 candidates
Feedback 3 → 7 candidates

The second type of guess may be more informative because its possible feedback values divide the remaining search space differently.

The implementation currently uses the number of distinct simulated feedback values as a simple entropy-like heuristic:

The goal is therefore no longer simply:

> "Find a permutation that might be correct."

It becomes:

> "Find a permutation whose feedback could help distinguish between the remaining possibilities."

This was the first point where the project began moving from heuristic guessing toward information-driven search.

---

# What Worked, And What Broke

The agents performed well on the original four-element problem.

This was encouraging, but it also created a misleading impression of how effective the strategies actually were.

The search space was:

4! = 24

At ten elements, it becomes:

10! = 3,628,800

The increase is not linear.

The number of possible states explodes as the number of elements increases.

As the puzzle grew, the weaknesses of the existing approaches became much more apparent:

* Local swaps explore only a small portion of the search space.
* Random sampling becomes increasingly inefficient.
* A high score does not necessarily identify useful positional information.
* Remembering previous guesses does not automatically produce useful reasoning.
* Candidate pools are only samples of the actual hypothesis space.
* The current entropy heuristic is based on the candidate pool rather than the complete set of remaining possibilities.

The Hybrid Agent therefore represents the best of the current heuristic approaches, but not necessarily the best algorithm for the problem.

---

# The Turning Point

At this stage, repeatedly modifying the agents began to feel less useful.

The experiments had revealed that the problem was not simply a matter of adding more memory, more swaps, or more random sampling.

Rather than continuing to patch the Hybrid Agent, the project will step back and investigate algorithms designed for:

* constraint satisfaction,
* permutation search,
* Mastermind-style problems,
* information gain,
* minimax search,
* and partially observable decision-making.

The objective is to develop a more principled solver that takes advantage of the mathematical structure of the puzzle.

The existing agents will remain valuable as baselines.

They are not failed experiments to be discarded; they establish a progression:

Random
   ↓
Score-based heuristics
   ↓
Memory
   ↓
Constraint consistency
   ↓
Local + global search
   ↓
Information-based candidate selection
   ↓
Principled constraint / information search

The next experiment will therefore focus less on adding complexity to the existing agents and more on understanding what information the feedback provides, how that information constrains the hidden permutation, and how an agent can choose actions that maximize what it learns from each move.
